\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Finite-order expansions and global effective laws}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{The estimates in \ref{fdip-00GN} hold at each fixed order,
on a specified compact retained set and bounded nonnegative
coupling interval. Their constants depend on that set and order.
They do not supply a bound for all retained fields, a convergent
infinite coupling series, or a complex coupling neighborhood.}

\p{The three-coordinate quartic in \ref{fdip-0047} exhibits a concrete
failure of global use of the truncation. Its second-order effective
polynomial has a negative leading sextic term, and that example
shows that exponentiating it against the retained Gaussian gives
an infinite integral for every positive coupling. The exact reduced
weight in \ref{fdip-00GJ} is instead at most #{\ue^{-\lambda x^4/24}}.
The compact-set remainder estimate is consistent with both facts:
it places no restriction on the truncation as #{|x|\to\infty}.
A polynomial approximation intended as a probability density needs
its own global stability and error estimates.}

\p{The exact iteration theorem \ref{fdip-00GK} preserves the entire
positive weight and every scalar normalizer. Generated higher
coefficients in \ref{fdip-0046} and \ref{fdip-0060} therefore do
not justify exact quartic or finite-parameter closure. Likewise,
[connected correlations](fdip-00GM) are derivatives of a source
logarithm; a one-particle irreducible effective action instead uses
a Legendre transform, as in
\citet{v2, Proposition 3.23, p. 50}{etingof2024mathematical}.
A fiber logarithm, a source logarithm and that Legendre transform
perform different operations.}
